(1) F-Strings:-
F-String was introduced in Python 3.6, and is now the preferred way of formatting strings.
To specify a string as an f-string, simply put an f in front of the string literal, and
add curly brackets {} as placeholders for variables and other operations.

###Example
Create an f-string:
Code:-
age = 36
txt = f"My name is John, I am {age}"
print(txt)
Output:- My name is John, I am 36

(2) format() method:-
python code:-
age = 36
txt = "My name is John, I am {}".format(age)
print(txt)
Output:- My name is John, I am 36
(3) Convert explicitly with str()
python code:-
age = 36
txt = "My name is John, I am " + str(age)
print(txt)
Output:- My name is John, I am 36

(4) Placeholders and Modifiers:-
A placeholder can contain variables, operations, functions, and modifiers to format the value.

###Example:-
Add a placeholder for the price variable:
COde:-
price = 59                              ###{price} is a placeholder – it gets replaced by the current value of the variable price.
                                            The f before the string tells Python to format the string by evaluating the expressions inside {}
txt = f"The price is {price} dollars"
print(txt)
Output:- The price is 59 dollars

(5) Modifiers (format specifiers) inside the placeholder:-
A modifier is included by adding a colon : followed by a legal formatting type, like .2f which means fixed point number with 2 decimals:

###Example with modifier
python code:-
price = 59.9876
txt = f"The price is {price:.2f} dollars"
print(txt)
Output:- The price is 59.99 dollars

#NOTE:-Here :.2f is the modifier:

(a) : starts the format specifier

(b) .2 means 2 decimal places

(c) f means fixed-point number
